From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Benjamin Gregoire <Benjamin.Gregoire@inria.fr>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 12:02:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Memory and array accesses in two modes: with errors, or total

A class SemMode (utils.v) with two instances, partial (the default: every
existing use keeps its meaning) and total, selects the mode of the byte
accesses get and set of coreMem, hence of read, write, WArray.get, set,
get_sub, set_sub and copy, whose definitions are unchanged but for the
guard (is_total || ...). In the total mode no access fails: a byte that
cannot be read (out of bounds, not initialised, not allocated) reads as 0,
and a write is effective on the domain set_dom of the memory: everywhere
for the memory (set_dom_mem, so that get (set m x v) y is v when x = y and
get m y otherwise), inside the bounds for an array (a write outside them
leaves the array unchanged).

The class carries the axioms of the total mode (totality, agreement with
the partial mode on its successes, setP_total relative to set_dom) next to
the unchanged axioms of the partial mode; the two instances satisfy them.
read_partialE and write_partialE state that the partial mode is the total
mode under its guard (validr, validw). The Ocopy lemmas of sopn.v and
sopn_facts.v use them.

The OCaml code that reads arrays (conversion, evaluator, variable
allocation) passes the partial mode explicitly.

(cherry picked from commit 132997dd9b4571f9e783bc9b576bb2af0f6b3a7a)
